 Attempted to overwrite out buffer while outputting a symbol.\nret0 Attempted to write before out buffer (invalid backwards distance).\nret0 Detected unknown block type within input stream.\nret0 Failed to find LEN and NLEN as complements within stored (uncompressed) stream.\nret0 Stored block extends beyond end of input stream.\n```\n\nThe fuzzers add `SIGSEGV` (a stored block whose `LEN` runs past the output\nmapping — `cp_stored` has no output bounds check).\n\n## D. Conditions that are **C undefined behaviour**\n\n`lib.c` performs a few operations whose observable effect depends on stack\nlayout / stack garbage rather than on the input, so there is no defined C result\nfor the Rust port to reproduce. They are **detected mechanically** by the\nindependent model (`tests/common/cmodel.rs`), which flags:\n\n| C operation (UB) | source | detected as |\n|------------------|--------|-------------|\n| `lens[n]` written for `n >= 288 + 32` — a `16`/`17`/`18` run overshooting `nlit + ndst` writes up to 137 bytes past `uint8_t lens[288+32]` | `lib.c:230-250` | `cp_dynamic: lens[n] written for n >= 320, …` |\n| `lens[-1]` read when the first code-length symbol is `16` (uninitialised stack) | `lib.c:234-237` | `cp_dynamic: lens[-1] read (uninitialised stack)` |\n| `counts[lens[n]]++` with `lens[n] >= 16` (past `int counts[16]`) | `lib.c:142-143` | `cp_build: counts[N]++ past int counts[16]` |\n| `cp_len_extra_bits[symbol-257]` / `cp_dist_*[dsym]` read past the tables when a garbage Huffman key decodes outside `257..=287` / `0..=31` | `lib.c:273-277` | `cp_block: cp_len_extra_bits[N] past uint8_t[29+2]` |\n\nInputs flagged this way are **counted and reported**, not compared:\n\n* truncation sweep: 72 of 7 560 inputs (0.95 %),\n* random fuzzer: 23 of 1 200, structured fuzzer: 45 of 1 500,\n* bit-flip fuzzer: 0, truncation fuzzer: 0.\n\nEach fuzz row additionally asserts that fewer than 25 % of its inputs are\nUB-tainted, so the rows cannot \"pass\" by skipping everything. The **7 488\nwell-defined sweep inputs and all 4 400+ well-defined fuzz inputs produce\nbyte-identical results in the C `.so`, the Rust `.so` and the model.**\n\nTwo further C-UB conditions are simply not fed to the libraries, because their\n\"result\" is compiler-dependent rather than merely stack-dependent:\n\n* `unfilter`: `w * bpp` signed-overflow, and any `w/h/bpp` that makes the\n  scanline walk leave the caller's allocation by more than the test padding;\n* mutating `cp_permutation_order` to a value `> 18` (`lenlens[order[i]]` would\n  index past `uint8_t lenlens[19]`), so `i29_mutate_permutation_order` only ever\n  installs genuine permutations of `0…18`.\n\nNote that `cp_inflate` reading `in[i]` outside `0..in_bytes` (the unaligned head,\nthe final partial word and `cp_stored`'s source pointer) is *not* in this list:\nthe harness hands the **same** page-guarded, zero-filled mapping to both\nlibraries, so those reads are perfectly deterministic and are compared.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":11,"oldLines":78,"newStart":11,"newLines":128,"lines":[" `__assert_fail()` → message on `stderr` + `SIGABRT` (signal 6). The Rust port"," reproduces this through `src/cassert.rs`."," ","-Message comparison note: glibc prints `<prog>: <__FILE__>:<line>: <func>:","-Assertion `<expr>' failed.` and CMake compiles with an **absolute** `__FILE__`,","-so the differential tests normalise the path prefix (everything up to and","-including the last `lib.c`) and compare `:<line>: <func>: Assertion `<expr>'","-failed.` verbatim, plus the termination signal.","+## How the error paths are compared"," ","-`cp_error_reason` is compared as a NUL-terminated C string read out of each","-`.so` via `dlsym`, so the exact message text is part of every error assertion.","+Every call goes through `tests/common/mod.rs`:"," ","+* the call runs in a **forked child** whose output buffer is a `MAP_SHARED`","+  mapping surrounded by `PROT_NONE` guard pages, so an over/underrun becomes a","+  deterministic `SIGSEGV` at the same offset in both libraries;","+* the **same** input mapping (zero-filled outside the stream) is handed to both","+  libraries, so even the C code's deliberate out-of-bounds head/tail reads see","+  identical bytes;","+* compared per call: `WTERMSIG`, exit status, return value, the","+  NUL-terminated `cp_error_reason` string read back through `dlsym`, the whole","+  output mapping, and the child's `stderr`;","+* `stderr` normalisation: glibc prints `<prog>: <__FILE__>:<line>: <func>:","+  Assertion `<expr>' failed.` and CMake compiles with an **absolute**","+  `__FILE__`, so everything up to the last `lib.c` is stripped and","+  `lib.c:<line>: <func>: Assertion `<expr>' failed.` is compared verbatim;","+* an independent transcription of `lib.c` (`tests/common/cmodel.rs`) is a third","+  opinion on every input, so a row cannot pass because both libraries are","+  *equally* wrong about what the C source says.","+"," ## A. `assert()` rejections"," "," | # | function (line) | trigger (exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test | ✔ |"," |---|-----------------|------------------------------------------|-------------------|------|---|","-| E1 | `cp_ptr` (95) `!(s->bits_left & 7)` | stored block whose header is decoded while `bits_left` is not a multiple of 8. Reached only after `cp_peak_bits` loaded the *final partial word* at a moment when the consumed-bit count was not a multiple of 8, which desynchronises `count` from `bits_left`; concretely `[0x02,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00]` style streams where a stored block follows a static block near EOF. | `SIGABRT`, `lib.c:95: cp_ptr: Assertion \\`!(s->bits_left & 7)' failed.` | `inflate_errors.rs::e1_cp_ptr_unaligned_bits_left` | [x] |","-| E2 | `cp_peak_bits` (104) `s->word_index <= s->word_count` | **provably unreachable**: `word_index` is incremented only inside `if (s->word_index < s->word_count)`, so after `++` it is `<= word_count`. No input can trigger it. | n/a | `inflate_errors.rs::e2_unreachable_word_index_invariant` (asserts the invariant holds over the whole randomized corpus, i.e. the assert never fires in either library) | [x] |","-| E3 | `cp_consume_bits` (115) `s->count >= num_bits_to_read` | Huffman code whose length exceeds the number of buffered bits at EOF: truncated static block, e.g. 1-byte input `[0x03]` → `cp_decode` consumes a 7-bit code with `count == 5`. | `SIGABRT`, `lib.c:115: cp_consume_bits: Assertion \\`s->count >= num_bits_to_read' failed.` | `inflate_errors.rs::e3_consume_bits_underflow` | [x] |","-| E4 | `cp_read_bits` (123) `num_bits_to_read <= 32` | `cp_len_extra_bits` / `cp_dist_extra_bits` are **writable exported globals**; setting `cp_len_extra_bits[0] = 33` and decoding length symbol 257 passes 33 to `cp_read_bits`. (No non-mutating input can reach it: all call sites pass 0…16 or `count & 7`.) | `SIGABRT`, `lib.c:123: cp_read_bits: Assertion \\`num_bits_to_read <= 32' failed.` | `inflate_errors.rs::e4_num_bits_gt_32_via_global` | [x] |","-| E5 | `cp_read_bits` (124) `num_bits_to_read >= 0` | **unreachable through the ABI**: the argument is either a literal (1,2,3,4,5,7,16), `s->count & 7` (≥ 0 because `count` is never negative), or a `uint8_t` table entry (0…255 → never negative as an `int`). | n/a | `inflate_errors.rs::e5_unreachable_negative_num_bits` (documents + checks that the 0-bit boundary `num_bits_to_read == 0`, the closest reachable value, behaves identically) | [x] |","-| E6 | `cp_read_bits` (125) `s->bits_left > 0` | input exhausted: `cp_inflate(in, 0, out, n)` (`bits_left == 0` on the very first `cp_read_bits(s,1)`); also any negative `in_bytes`. | `SIGABRT`, `lib.c:125: cp_read_bits: Assertion \\`s->bits_left > 0' failed.` | `inflate_errors.rs::e6_zero_and_negative_in_bytes` | [x] |","-| E7 | `cp_read_bits` (126) `s->count <= 64` | **unreachable**: `cp_peak_bits` only tops up when `count < num_bits_to_read <= 32`, so `count <= 32 + 31 = 63` after a word load; the final-word path adds `bits_left`, a